{
  "title": "A game that ends without a winner leaves the match live, and its next game lands in the finished game's directory",
  "description": "MECHANISM, from source and from the corpus on disk. MatchImpl.endGame \
credits a win only `if (player.hasWon())`, and MatchImpl.checkIfMatchEnds ends the \
match only when some player's wins reach winsNeeded (or fewer than two players remain). \
A game that finishes with NOBODY marked as having won -- a deck-out, an effect, a concession, or a genuine draw; anything \
where neither seat is marked as having won -- therefore credits no win, the match is not over, and \
TableController:855 immediately calls startGame again. That next game inherits the \
match's gameLogDir, which is the directory of the game that just finished, so the \
server appends a second game's events into it while the sequential runner has already \
moved its observer to the next table. THE ENGINE IS BEHAVING CORRECTLY for a \
best-of-N match; what is wrong is a harness that plays one game per table and never \
tells the server the match is done. EVIDENCE. Step-1 corpus, 3,964 game directories, \
10 hold two games (0.25%). The two games never OVERLAP in time -- the second begins \
1.1s after the first ends in 8 of 10, 0.1s in one -- which is what ruled out a \
directory race between workers, since two racing writers overlap by definition. Every \
affected session produced 5 distinct sibling directories (_s1.._s5), so claim_game_dir \
worked in every case. The second game carries a THIRD game id, belonging to neither \
the finished game nor the next table's game, so it is not a misdirected write but a \
game nobody was watching. Nine of the ten first games ended with NO player at or below \
0 life, against 583 of 600 sampled normal games ending on a kill; three of the ten ended during the \
DRAW STEP, which is a deck-out and not a drawn game -- `step` is the turn step, not \
an outcome. NOT SUFFICIENT, ONLY NECESSARY: 12 of those 600 \
normal games also ended without a kill and did NOT double, which is consistent with \
the second game only appearing if it starts before the session tears the server down. \
So the rate is a function of timing, not of worker count -- it does not fall with \
--parallel and does not gate raising it. An earlier claim that it scaled with \
concurrency was mine and is withdrawn. FIX: the keepAlive observer now quits the \
previous match before creating the next table (quitPreviousMatch, tracking the watched \
game id), so the match ends with its game. The runner additionally reports any \
directory that received a second game at session end -- checked at the END because a \
check straight after game_end would pass, the second game arriving ~1.1s later. Loud \
rather than fatal: the games are already on disk and failing the session would not \
unwrite them; what matters is seeing it at generation rather than inferring it at \
assembly from a provenance field that is right for one of the two games. The detector \
was validated against the real corpus, not only fixtures: 3,964 directories scanned, \
exactly the 10 known cases found. \
\
REOPENED 2026-08-25. THE FIX DID NOT WORK, AND THE MECHANISM ABOVE IS PARTLY WRONG. \
Block 2 (pin f0008f6d, quitPreviousMatch in place) produced games/step1-b2/lascar/logs/game_20260825_060344_s2, seed 921507, with two game_start and two game_end. Measured over block 2 so far: 3 doubled of 1,680 directories (0.179%) WITH the fix, against 10 of 3,964 (0.252%) without it. Fisher one-sided p=0.428 -- no evidence of any reduction, and rates up to 0.46% are still consistent with 3/1,680. Treat the fix as unproven, not as partial. \
\
WHY IT MISSES, from the two logs, and it is a RACE THE FIX ENTERS TOO LATE rather than a quit that fails. Session session_20260825_060344, one keepAlive observer: \
  09:05:01.536  keepAlive receives ONE command for gameDir _s2, gameSeed 921507 \
  09:05:01.539  quitPreviousMatch for game 2c4b2edd (the PREVIOUS dir) -- works \
  09:05:01.696  table 3b8ca52f created; game b707e99b starts, seeded 921507 \
  09:05:31.700  game 23a28664 GameImpl.init, seeded 921507 AGAIN  <-- game 2 of the same match, started by the server the moment game 1 ended \
  09:05:31.809  quitPreviousMatch for b707e99b  <-- 109 ms TOO LATE \
The quit fires when the NEXT command arrives, and the server starts the next game of the live match before that command can get there. Sibling seeds are 921506/921507/ 921508/921509, one per directory, so both games seeded 921507 belong to _s2 and the second is not a misdirected write from a neighbour. \
\
THE `hasWon()` STORY IS RIGHT; THE `no winner` STORY IS NOT. This instance\'s FIRST game_end carries winner=Skill8, life {Skill8: 17, Skill1: 7} -- a credited winner in the event, with the loser alive at 7 life. The event\'s winner is DERIVED, not hasWon(): ServerGameEventLogCollector says so in its own comment -- \"hasWon() is only set for explicit win the game effects... fall back to: if exactly one player hasn\'t lost, they won\". So the title\'s \"without a winner\" describes the event field, which is the wrong field, and \"nine of ten ended with no player at or below 0 life\" is a correlate rather than the mechanism. WHAT IS STILL UNEXPLAINED, and is the next thing to establish: if hasWon() is false for nearly every normal ending, the match should fail to end nearly every time, yet 3,961 of 3,964 directories hold one game. checkIfMatchEnds has a second exit -- activePlayers < 2 via matchPlayer.hasQuit() -- so the discriminator is most likely whether the LOSER leaves the match, not whether the winner won. Not verified. Do not write a fix against the hasWon() branch until it is. \
\
DIRECTION, unimplemented: end the match when its game ends rather than when the next command arrives -- the observer already tracks watchedGameId, so quitting on the game-over callback closes the 109 ms window that this instance lost by. That is a change of WHEN, not of what. \
\
COUNT game_start, NOT game_end. Two of block 2\'s three doubled directories record game_start twice and game_end ONCE, so a game_end count finds 1 of 3. The runner\'s own detector (dirs_holding_more_than_one_game) already counts game_start and is correct; the undercount is downstream, in mtg pipelines/synth/audit_step1.py, which takes `next(e for e in ev if type == game_end)` and therefore sees a doubled directory as one complete game.",
